    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{7}^{x}}{{7}^{x}+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u={7}^{x}+4.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={7}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\,\\text{d}u={7}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{7}^{x}}{{7}^{x}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{7}^{x}+4}\\cdot {7}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{7}^{x}+4|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{x}+4)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we used the fact that ${7}^{x}+4>0$ for all $x.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/id": "5757ffb4-f2e5-5268-bd6c-8d2bb3a86334",
